    With all the suggestions for improvements posted, I feel obligated to
offer my own.  When I was in high school (1984 or so), the school acquired
a terminal connected to CDC's Plato system.  The terminal was intended for
educational purposes.  No one used the terminal much until someone discovered
the online games.  There were several dungeon games similar to hack.  The
game that generated the most interest (also the most trouble) was called
Avatar.  This is a multi-level, graphics-based, multi-user D&D type game.
The game has many features that I would like to see in a future version of
hack.  A personal version of Avatar would even be better--but that's wishful
thinking.

    The feature that I liked most was the real-time multi-user design.
Players can:
	*   talk and send messages to each other
	*   join forces and go on group missions
	*   rescue each other in times of trouble

    A multiuser hack would require either a multiuser system or networked
single-user systems (could be as simple as PC's connected by modem).

    One requirement for a multiuser hack is that the dungeon maps should be
somehow fixed among a group of users.

    Other features of Avatar that I like are:

	*   The maze is shown in 3-D.  You see what is directly in
	    front of you and have no overhead view of the dungeon
	    (more realistic).

	*   Monsters can occur in groups, i.e:
		You see a group of 5 silent eagles.

	*   The top level (above ground) contains a store, a morgue, and
	    (I think) a hotel.

	*   You can purchase and sell items in the store.  The contents of
	    store depends on what is sold and bought by previous characters.

	*   Items have their own alignment: good, neutral, evil.  Their
	    effectiveness depends on the alignment of the user.

	*   You can charm monsters and have them fight side-by-side and
	    follow your every step.

	*   Monsters are graphically displayed on the screen when
	    encountered.

	*   Wands are not the only magic-casting items.  There are staves and
	    other weapons that can cast magic.

	*   Dead characters can be resurrected with a loss of constitution
	    and other possible bad effects.

	*   Character stats are initially rolled as in D&D.

	*   A character can be one of several races (human, elf, orc, etc.).
	    Once a character has enough experience and gold, and be of the
	    proper alignment, he can choose to join a guild: thief, wizard,
	    healer, etc.  A character can join more than one guild, but his
	    (her) level is specific to a particular guild.  (One can be a
	    3rd level thief and a 10th level mage, for example.)

    That's about all I can remember about Avatar.  I would like to hear from
anyone who knows about the current availability of Avatar.  I would like at
least to know whether Avatar still exists.

    Another game has a shop where one can buy and sell tame monsters.  The
monster that you buy may turn wild in time if its level is higher than yours.
The cost depends on the number hit points of the creature.

